# Waves and Sound: Oscillations That Carry Energy

> A wave carries energy, not matter. This lesson covers wave
> vocabulary, the behaviour of sound, echo ranging, SONAR, the
> Doppler effect, and sonic booms.

## 1. What Is a Wave?

A **wave** is a disturbance that travels carrying energy without
carrying matter along — a floating cork bobs in place as ripples
pass. In a **transverse** wave the particles oscillate across the
direction of travel; in a **longitudinal** wave they oscillate
along it.

| Feature | Transverse | Longitudinal |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Particle motion | perpendicular to travel | parallel to travel |
| Shape | crests and troughs | compressions and rarefactions |
| Examples | rope wave, water ripples, light | sound, spring pulses |

@diagram tree id="wave-classification" layout="tree" aria="Waves classified as transverse or longitudinal with examples"
  "Wave" -> "Transverse: rope, ripples, light"
  "Wave" -> "Longitudinal: sound, spring pulses"
@/diagram

## 2. Wave Vocabulary

**Amplitude** is the maximum disturbance, **wavelength** λ the
length of one full wave, **frequency** f the waves passing per
second, and **time period** T = 1/f.

@math label="eq:wave-speed"
v = f\lambda
@/math

[example]
A 500 Hz note in air at 343 m/s has
λ = 343 ÷ 500 ≈ 0.69 m — the spacing between successive
compressions.
[/example]

## 3. Sound: A Mechanical Wave

Sound is a **longitudinal mechanical wave** — it needs a medium
and cannot cross a vacuum, as the classic bell-jar experiment
shows. Speed rises with the medium's stiffness:

@chart bar title="Approximate speed of sound (m/s)"
labels:
Air 20 °C
Water
Steel

data:
343
1500
5000
@/chart

## 4. Pitch, Loudness, and Quality

| Sensation | Depends on | Change |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Pitch | frequency | higher f → shriller note |
| Loudness | amplitude | larger amplitude → louder |
| Quality (timbre) | waveform | separates a flute from a violin |

The healthy human ear hears roughly **20 Hz to 20,000 Hz**.

## 5. Infrasound and Ultrasound

**Infrasound** (below 20 Hz) is produced by earthquakes and
severe storms — animals often sense it before humans feel the
quake. **Ultrasound** (above 20,000 Hz) reflects finely off small
objects, which suits it to **SONAR**, medical sonography, breaking
kidney stones, and the echolocation of bats and dolphins.

[note]
Ultrasound is chosen for scans because it is harmless compared
with X-rays, and its tiny wavelength resolves fine detail.
[/note]

## 6. Echo and Reverberation

An **echo** is a distinct reflected sound. The ear holds a
sensation about 0.1 s, so the reflector must be at least

@math label="eq:echo-distance"
d = \frac{v t}{2} = \frac{343 \times 0.1}{2} \approx 17.2\ \mathrm{m}
@/math

away for the echo to be heard separately. **Reverberation** — the
persistence of sound from many reflections — blurs speech in a
bare hall, so walls and ceilings are covered with absorbers.

## 7. SONAR

**SONAR** (sound navigation and ranging) times an ultrasonic pulse
to the sea floor and back:

@math label="eq:sonar"
d = \frac{v \times t}{2}
@/math

[example]
A pulse returns after 2 s; sea water carries sound at 1500 m/s,
so the depth d = 1500 × 2 ÷ 2 = 1500 m.
[/example]

## 8. Doppler Effect and Shock Waves

The **Doppler effect** raises the pitch of a source approaching
you and lowers it as it recedes — the siren of a passing
ambulance. The same shift in radar speed guns and in the redshift
of galaxies reveals motion along the line of sight.

@math label="eq:doppler"
f' = f\,\frac{v \pm v_{o}}{v \mp v_{s}}
@/math

When a source outruns its own sound (speed > Mach 1), the
wavefronts pile into a cone and produce a **sonic boom**. The
**Mach number** is the ratio of source speed to sound speed.

## 9. Quick Revision

| Item | Remember |
| --- | --- |
| Wave relation | v = fλ |
| Sound in air | ~343 m/s at 20 °C; faster in water, fastest in steel |
| Sound needs | a medium — never a vacuum |
| Audible range | 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz |
| Echo rule | reflector ≥ 17.2 m away (0.1 s persistence) |
| SONAR depth | d = vt ÷ 2 |
| Sonic boom | source exceeding the speed of sound (Mach > 1) |
